{"text": "Lucas learns most successfully through predictable sequence and small first step; co-regulation before problem-solving; brief supported exposure to avoided tasks; and specific feedback on coping strategies. These strategies make instruction clearer and reduce the amount of energy spent simply gaining access.\n\nThe profile also identifies predictable barriers. Lucas may struggle with transitions into school; public correction or surprise performance; uncertain multi-step tasks; and returning after escalation or absence. We should not treat these points as a list of personal deficits. Each one directs the team toward a response. For example, a barrier caused by pace may require additional processing time; an environmental barrier may require a quieter, safer, or more accessible setting; and task complexity may require modeling, chunking, direct instruction, or an alternate response.\n\nThe goal is not to remove every productive challenge. The goal is to remove barriers that prevent the student from engaging with the intended learning. Presenter 3 will now connect the profile’s sensory, medical, behavioural, and safety information to the EA’s day-to-day responsibilities.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-R-04.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Lucas", "start": 0.1125, "end": 0.55, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 5}, {"text": "learns", "start": 0.5625, "end": 0.9375, "start_char": 6, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "most", "start": 0.95, "end": 1.2125, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 17}, {"text": "successfully", "start": 1.225, "end": 1.925, "start_char": 18, "end_char": 30}, {"text": "through", "start": 1.9375, "end": 2.15, "start_char": 31, "end_char": 38}, {"text": "predictable", "start": 2.1625, "end": 2.725, "start_char": 39, "end_char": 50}, {"text": "sequence", "start": 2.7375, "end": 3.3, "start_char": 51, "end_char": 59}, {"text": "and", "start": 3.3125, "end": 3.4625, "start_char": 60, "end_char": 63}, {"text": "small", "start": 3.475, "end":